Here is the short answer: yes, and it may be the most beautiful thing on Luxor's west bank. Medinet Habu is the mortuary temple of Ramesses III, and it holds more surviving colour than almost any temple of its size in Egypt. Entry is about 220 EGP — roughly four euros — and most mornings you can have whole courtyards to yourself.

Ahmed grew up fifteen minutes from its gate. He takes guests here when he wants them to understand what these temples actually looked like three thousand years ago — not bare honey-coloured stone, but painted, every surface, floor to ceiling.

## Why almost nobody is there

This is the honest part. Medinet Habu is not quiet because it's minor. It's quiet because it isn't on the coach itinerary.

The day-trip buses from Hurghada and the Red Sea have time for two or three stops before the long drive back. They go to the Valley of the Kings, they photograph Hatshepsut's temple from the car park, and they leave. Medinet Habu is a fifteen-minute detour that doesn't fit the schedule, so it gets cut.

That's the whole reason it's worth your morning. You get one of the finest temples in Egypt, and you get it the way the rest are impossible to get: nearly empty.

## What you're actually looking at

Medinet Habu was built as the mortuary temple of Ramesses III, the last of Egypt's great warrior pharaohs, around 1175 BC. Three things make it unusual.

### The colour

Most temples you'll see are bare stone — the paint burned off by three thousand years of sun. Here, because so much of the complex stayed buried or sheltered, the pigment survived. Look up, into the ceilings and the undersides of the gateways, and you'll see blues, reds and golds that haven't faded. It is the closest most people ever come to seeing one of these buildings as it was meant to look.

### The fortress gate

You enter through a high, fortified gatehouse built in the style of a Syrian migdol fort — more like a castle than a temple. Ramesses III had reason to build for defence. Which brings us to the walls.

### The battle reliefs

The outer walls are carved with his wars, including his defeat of the Sea Peoples — one of the most detailed war records left from the ancient world, down to the counted hands and the prisoners. It is history written to be believed, by the man who won.

## How to do it properly

- **Go at opening, around 6am.** The reliefs take raking morning light beautifully, the stone is cool, and the quiet is the point. By late morning it's hot and whatever few groups come have arrived.
- **Give it 1.5 to 2 hours.** It's bigger than it looks from the gate, and the detail rewards slow looking.
- **Look up, and look in the shadows.** The best-preserved colour is where the sun couldn't reach it.
- **Pair it with the Valley at dawn.** The Valley of the Kings opens at six and empties of its best hour by nine. Do the Valley first, then Medinet Habu while it's still cool — the correct order for a west-bank morning, and one almost nobody gets right. We lay this out in [our guide to the Valley of the Kings](/insiders-guide/valley-of-the-kings-which-tombs).

## What most people miss

The far corners. Most of the handful of visitors who do come stop at the first great court and turn back. Keep going — the rear halls, the smaller chambers, the chapels off to the sides. That's where the ceilings still have their stars, and where you'll stand completely alone.

This is the kind of thing that's hard to know on your own and easy to arrange: which gate, which hour, which corner, and a guide who can actually read the walls for you rather than reciting a script. It's the difference between walking past three thousand years of colour and being shown it. If you'd rather have the morning handled, [we build the day around it](/concierge-day).
